A||N||2||87FieroGT||Possible clutch problems!!!||||11
Z||000000||87FieroGT||11-13-1999||10:24 AM||||Ever since I bought my 87 GT I had to get the clutch working first. To do so I replaced all components for the hydraulics. The master, slave, and clutch line, but my pedal effort has always been hard even though I removed all the air from the system. The clutch works good but sometimes it is hard to shift gears when it gets hot outside. I was looking at an 86 GT a while back that had the clutch replaced on it and the pedal was much smoother than mine. My main question is if it is time for my actual clutch to be replaced? There is no slipping of the clutch when driving at speed; should I let it be or go through the struggle of replacing the clutch just to get the right pedal feel. The car now has 72,000 original miles and runs good and strong. Any help would be appreciated. Thanks.||||reg||11
Z||000001||Carrolles||11-15-1999||07:34 AM||||If the clutch pedal seems to bind when you push in the pedal it could be a throwout bearing that is sticking to the shaft and not sliding correctly.||||reg||1
Z||000002||Phil||11-15-1999||08:58 AM||||A somewhat easy way to check to see if your throw out bearing or fork shaft is binding is to pull the slave push rod back ( use your imagination as to how) and then see if clutch arm moves freely. That's alot easier than going inside to look. ||||reg||1
